Engine: one command per battle action, MSG_COMBAT_COMMAND (open fix 58).

STATUS: APPLIED 30 September 2026 by work order 194 (Part B; reserved by
work order 193) — orion2re e4b2256a on orionlayer-local (before: 5bd4c404), this
file's diff byte for byte (index lines included). Required by
tools/version_check.py since work order 194.

It was applied, from ~/orion2re on the orionlayer-local branch, with:

    patch -p1 < ~/orionlayerv3/doc/ext_combat_commands.patch

and a rebuild. It comes back off with `git revert e4b2256a` or `patch -R -p1`,
and a rebuild.

WHAT IT IS FOR

    Every battle action but the buttons is a click on the battle map, a
    single grid field whose cell the game takes from the pointer — shifted
    for large ships by the cursor IMAGE under it — at the original's view
    origin (combat1.cpp, the map branch); turning is a right click; a
    missile can be shot at only through its field inside the original's
    view. An HD battle cannot rely on any of that (work order 193 §4).

    MSG_COMBAT_COMMAND (0x88): battle serial, the acting unit, an op and up
    to three arguments — MOVE(cell), FIRE(target, slot mask), FIRE_MISSILE
    (missile), FACE(cell), SELECT(own unit), REMOVE_STASIS(unit),
    BOARD(target). It is consumed at ONE point, right after the human's
    Get_Input_ in COMBAT1::Do_Combat_Turn_ (ext::Take_Combat_Command): the
    command's input takes the click's place — the map field, its negative
    for FACE, a missile's field, the BOARD radio — and the map and face
    branches take the command's cell instead of the pointer's
    (ext::Combat_Command_Cell). Everything after that is the original's own
    code: the TACTICAL_NET_MSG_* it sends in a network game, Move_Ship_,
    Fire_Ship_, Turn_To_Face_, the bookkeeping, the stasis release's confirm
    box, the board popup. Checked first, and refused otherwise: the battle
    serial and the acting unit (a stale command is dropped), orders allowed
    now (_order_info_flag), and what the click branch itself relies on — a
    legal cell for MOVE, a live enemy at its anchor for FIRE, an own unit
    for SELECT (not in initiative mode), a unit in stasis for its release,
    a missile with a field. FIRE's slot mask is applied as the eight weapon
    rows would leave it — single player only: a network game learns the
    rows from TOGGLE_WEAPON messages, so there a mask other than "as
    switched" (-1) is refused. BOARD writes the BOARD radio's flag (the
    field engine does it on a click, an activation does not), lets the
    original's handler check Already_Boarded_, then takes the target on the
    next input. Taken or refused, the event stream says so (open fix 56's
    kind 18, with a reason).

    An engine without OrionLayer behaves exactly as before: with no command
    pending the three hooks return their input and cell unchanged. One
    marker, `OrionLayer, open fix 58.`, on one line at every changed
    place.

